// Handing a closure somewhere else to run.
//
// Two operations, and they are each other's halves: `spawn` starts work that
// must not block the caller, and `post` brings the answer back to the thread
// the target draws on. A UI program that does the first without the second has
// written a data race -- the widgets belong to that thread, and nothing else
// may touch them.
//
//	async.spawn(func() {
//	    var page = fetch(url)          // off the drawing thread
//	    async.post(func() { body = page })   // and back onto it
//	})
//
// **Each is answered by a different half of the build**, which is the whole
// reason they are two declarations rather than one. Starting work is a
// property of the host *language* -- a goroutine in Go, a microtask in
// JavaScript, a coroutine in Kotlin -- and none of that depends on what is
// being drawn. Reaching the drawing thread is a property of the *platform*:
// there is no such thread in general, and no two toolkits reach theirs the
// same way. fyne queues onto its driver, gtk4 onto a GLib idle tick, and a
// framework that owns its own loop may have no way in at all.
//
// So a target answers `spawn` on the language axis and `post` on the platform
// axis, and **a target that answers neither says so**: naming one it cannot
// emit is a build failure positioned at the call, rather than generated code
// that does not compile.
//
// The compiler writes both itself. A blocking call on a language that cannot
// suspend is moved to a spawn with its tail posted back -- that is
// `passAsyncOffload`, and it is why these two ids existed before this package
// did.
//
// What they were missing is a *name*. A platform reaches its own drawing
// thread from hand-written Go inside the generator, which is fine for as long
// as the generator is the only thing that needs to: the moment a platform's
// own `.sngl` describes a schedule -- a timer handing its tick to a host
// scheduler, and having to be back on the drawing thread before it touches a
// widget -- there is nothing for it to write but that platform's own spelling
// of `fyne.Do`, in its own package. That turns a hand-over every target needs
// into one platform's private affordance.
//
// That is also the line between these and the node operations a visual tree
// lowers to, which are deliberately *not* a package: nothing can name those,
// so a declaration would describe nobody's contract. These have a caller.
